<article class="env-definition" id="09JN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09JN">Definition <span data-tag="09JN">22.5.1</span>.</a> Let $(A, \text{d})$ be a differential graded algebra. Let $f, g : M \to N$ be homomorphisms of differential graded $A$-modules. A <em>homotopy between $f$ and $g$</em> is an $A$-module map $h : M \to N$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$h(M^ n) \subset N^{n - 1}$ for all $n$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f(x) - g(x) = \text{d}_ N(h(x)) + h(\text{d}_ M(x))$ for all $x \in M$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> If a homotopy exists, then we say $f$ and $g$ are <em>homotopic</em>. </p>
</article>